<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>





<handout>
    <title>Congruence</title>

    <page>
        
   
    <paragraphs>
        <title>Modular Arithmetic</title>

        <p>
            Modular arithmetic actually shows up lots of places - including in
            RSA Encryption (which is how your credit card number gets encrypted
            in an online transactions) and for <q>check digits</q> - a way to make
            sure a UPC bar code has scanned correctly.
            We introduce a lot of content in MTH 210 for the sake of learning
            how to write proofs, but modular arithmetic wins the award for
            <q> most likely to appear in your future classes </q>.
            It's worth investing the time to make sense of this topic!
        </p>

        <p>
            The metaphors from the text are a helpful of way understanding modular arithmetic,
            but we want to define the sandwich in a way that even when the
            metaphor fails us, we can still agree on what it means for two
            integers to be congruent.
        </p>

        </paragraphs>

        <definition>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    For <m>a,r\in\mathbb{Z}</m> and <m>m\in \mathbb{N}</m>, we
                    say that <m>a</m> is congruent to <m>r</m> modulo <m>m</m>,
                    and we write <m>a\equiv r \pmod{m}</m>, if <m>m\mid a-r</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <p>
            Note in Definition 2.14 of your text the author says <m>m</m> is an integer, but
            in practice, we really only think about <m>m</m> being a positive
            integer.
        </p>

        <activity>
            <title>Practice with congruence</title>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Find two integers <m>a</m> and <m>r</m> that are
                        congruent modulo <m>5</m>.
                        That is, give examples of <m>a</m> and <m>r</m> such
                        that <m>a\equiv r \pmod{5}</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Find all integers <m>x</m> such that
                        <m>x\equiv -2 \pmod{5}</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        True or false: If <m>x</m> is an integer, then there
                        exists a unique integer <m>y</m> with <m>0\le y\le 5</m>
                        such that <m>x\equiv y \pmod{5}</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        True or false: If <m>ak \equiv bk \pmod{m}</m> then
                        <m>a\equiv b\mod{m}</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </activity>

         </page>

         <page>

        <activity>
            <title>Direct Proofs with Modulo</title>
            
            
            <statement>
                <p>
                     Do a pre-proof analysis of the
                    following theorem.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </activity>

        <theorem>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    For all integers <m>a,b,c,</m> and <m>d</m>, and all natural
                    numbers <m>m</m>, if <m>a\equiv b \pmod{m}</m> and
                    <m>c\equiv d\pmod{m}</m> then
                    <m>a\cdot c \equiv b\cdot d\pmod{m}</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </theorem>

        <p>
            <em>Pre-proof analysis:</em>
        </p>
  

</page>
</handout>